MadGraph-owned Pythia8 main for beamstrahlung
=============================================

mg5amc_bstrahl.cc showers a dressed-lepton event file whose density
includes beamstrahlung, consuming the per-event

    <beamstrahlung> xbs1 xbs2 </beamstrahlung>

tag that MadGraph writes (see Template/Common/Source/PDF/sample_bstrahl.f).
Without it a shower attributes the whole of 1-x to ISR off a full-energy
beam and gives the hard system a recoil beamstrahlung does not produce.

Build
-----
Copy both files into the Pythia8 examples directory and

    make -f Makefile.mgbstrahl

Pythia's Makefile.inc supplies the flags, including HepMC, so the binary
matches the local Pythia build.  Nothing in Pythia's own Makefile is
touched.  For a read-only installation, copy them somewhere writable and

    make -f Makefile.mgbstrahl PYTHIA8=/path/to/pythia8

Pythia must be built with GZIP if the event file is compressed; the main
aborts rather than run on a file it could not scan, because leaving the
beams undegraded is exactly the wrong answer it exists to prevent.

Why it is a main and not just a BeamShape
-----------------------------------------
Two Pythia facts, both verified against 8.318 and both silent if ignored:

* Beams:allowVariableEnergy is read only for frameType < 4 (BeamSetup.cc),
  and setKinematics() aborts unless frameType == 2, so neither works with
  Les Houches input.  Beams:allowMomentumSpread is not gated on frameType,
  so a custom BeamShape is the way to degrade beams per event.  Measured:
  eCM then tracks sqrt(xbs1*xbs2*s) to 1e-12.

* Pythia never boosts an incoming Les Houches event inward -- BeamSetup::
  boostAndVertex is only ever called with toLab=true -- so it reads the LHE
  momenta as already being in the beams' CM.  Once the beams are degraded
  by different amounts that is not the frame MadGraph wrote, and the x<=1
  test in ProcessContainer ("setting mass failed") rejects the event.
  Measured on a 500 GeV ilc500ll sample: 58% of events rejected without the
  boost the LHAup applies, 0% with it.

The card
--------
MadGraph's own card uses names from the old MG5aMC_PY8_interface
(HEPMCoutput:file and friends), but banner.py's PY8Card.interface_to_164
table translates them before the card reaches a stock main, on both the
single-core and the parallelised path.  So this main sees Pythia's own
names only -- Main:HepMC and HepMC:output, with /dev/null meaning off --
exactly as main164 does, and registers nothing of its own.

Beams:LHEF does live inside a subrun block, so the card must be read as
readFile(card, 0) rather than as a flat file.  Only a single subrun is
supported: the beamstrahlung fractions belong to one event file, so
several subruns would need a set of fractions each.

Validated on 500 unmodified MadGraph events: 500 tried / 500 accepted,
Info::x1() equal to x/xbs to 5e-8 (the LHE momentum precision), eCM equal
to sqrt(xbs1*xbs2*s) to 1e-12, and no x above 1.  End to end through
generate_events on a parallelised (3-way split) run: every split 100/100
accepted, and Pythia's cross section 3.544e-10 mb against MadGraph's
0.3544 pb.

Not yet exercised: the HepMC output path, because the Pythia8 used for the
above was built without HepMC.

One-to-one showering
--------------------
The beams are chosen before the event is read, so the two must stay in
lockstep.  They do not if ProcessLevel rejects an event and reads another
within the same next() call, which would shower an event against another
event's beams.  The main checks the two counters every event and aborts
rather than write misassigned events.  Note that MadGraph declares its
event files with IDWTUP = -4 (weighted), which permits Pythia to consume
more than one event per next(); the guard is what makes that visible.

Physics validation
------------------
Showering the same 1000-event ilc500ll file with and without the beam
degradation, and splitting on whether the event actually had beamstrahlung
(pT of the mu+mu- system, which is zero before the shower):

                          <pT>     median    q75
  xbs=1  control, plain   15.07     0.617   10.46
  xbs=1  control, degrad  15.70     0.535   10.51
  xbs<1  signal,  plain   18.39     0.934   16.21
  xbs<1  signal,  degrad  14.43     0.499   10.22

Events that had no beamstrahlung are unaffected, as they must be.  Events
that did recoil visibly more when the loss is handed to the ISR shower, and
once the beams are degraded they fall back onto the control sample: q75
16.21 -> 10.22 against a control of 10.51.  That recoil is the artefact
this main removes.

Energy balance over the same sample:

  plain     sum(E_final) = 2*Ebeam          to 4.5e-11
  degraded  sum(E_final) = (y1+y2)*Ebeam    to 2.3e-08

i.e. with the beams degraded the beamstrahlung energy is correctly absent
from the event record, having left with the beam.

The size of the effect is set by the density: ilc500ll loses on average
under 1% per beam, so the shift is modest.  A machine with harder
beamstrahlung would show more.
